<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerard of Csanád</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–<a href="/years/1046">1046</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Gerard Sagredo, Saint Gellért</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerard of Csanád was a Venetian-born Benedictine monk who became tutor to Stephen I's son Emeric and, around 1030, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Csanád (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenad">Cenad</a>) in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> during the pagan uprising of 1046, according to legend thrown from the hill now named after him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Canonised in 1083, he is venerated as a patron saint of Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad.jpg" alt="Gerard of Csanád" width="320" height="725" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Rocco_(Venice)_-_Statue_of_Saint_Gerard_of_Csan%C3%A1d.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen I was canonised in 1083, together with his son Emeric and Bishop <b>Gerard of Csanád</b>, and Ladislaus I in 1192; the dynasty became known for its saints, including Andrew II's daughter Elizabeth of Hungary and Béla IV's daughter Margaret.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1022" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gerard of Csanád"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1022">What was here in 1022? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
